# ── Cloudflare Pages routing — READ BEFORE EDITING ──────────────────────────
#
# Pages does NOT honour specific 200 rewrite rules to /index.html for paths
# with no file on disk. `/editor*  /index.html  200` silently fails: the rule
# looks correct, Pages ignores it, and the request falls through to Pages' own
# behaviour, which 308s to `/`. That is what sent /editor, /login, /home,
# /library, /projects, /shared, /analytics, /settings, /claim, /dashboard,
# /video-editor and /v/:id to the marketing page instead of the app.
#
# Pages also does NOT support a 404 status in this file. `/*  /index.html  404`
# is silently downgraded to 200.
#
# The only configuration that works is these three things, in this order:
#
#   1. 301 redirects for normalisation      — these do work, reliably
#   2. Prerendered files served from disk    — automatic, needs no rule
#   3. One `/*  /index.html  200` catch-all  — everything else
#
# DO NOT ADD A 404.html FILE. Its presence flips Pages out of the SPA
# assumption: with no 404.html, an unmatched path serves index.html and React
# Router renders the route; with one, Pages 308-redirects unmatched paths to
# `/` instead, which breaks every client-side route that has no file on disk.
# vite.config.ts used to emit one — that is why it no longer does.
#
# The cost of this arrangement is that an unknown URL answers 200 with the SPA
# shell rather than a 404, so /nonexistent is a soft 404. src/pages/NotFound.tsx
# is what contains it: the catch-all route renders it with `noIndex`, which
# emits `robots: noindex, nofollow` and suppresses the canonical tag, so the
# soft 404 is not indexable. That is the trade Pages leaves available — a
# working app, or honest status codes, not both.
#
# Rule order matters: Pages takes the first match, so every 301 has to precede
# the catch-all or the catch-all swallows it.

# ── Consolidated posts — 301 to the landing page that now owns the keyword ──
#
# These two posts competed head-on with their own landing pages for the same
# commercial-intent queries, splitting authority three ways. Both were removed
# from blogData.ts, which also drops them from the generated sitemap and the
# prerender list. They must sit ABOVE the /blog/:slug rule below, or that rule
# claims them first and they end up at /blog/ via the client-side redirect.
# Both the bare and trailing-slash forms are listed because either can be linked.
/blog/snaprec-vs-loom-free-alternative   /loom-alternative/           301
/blog/snaprec-vs-loom-free-alternative/  /loom-alternative/           301
/blog/screencastify-vs-snaprec-free-alternative   /screencastify-alternative/  301
/blog/screencastify-vs-snaprec-free-alternative/  /screencastify-alternative/  301

# ── Trailing slash — explicit 301 to the canonical form ─────────────────────
#
# The sitemap, every internal link and every canonical tag use the slashed
# form; these make the bare form agree instead of leaving both in front of
# Googlebot. Every destination here is a prerendered file on disk — the parity
# test in src/__tests__/routes.test.ts is what keeps that true.
/blog/:slug  /blog/:slug/  301
/about/ghulam-muhammad  /about/ghulam-muhammad/  301
/blog  /blog/  301
/about  /about/  301
/how-it-works  /how-it-works/  301
/changelog  /changelog/  301
/privacy  /privacy/  301
/terms  /terms/  301
/contact  /contact/  301
/loom-alternative  /loom-alternative/  301
/screencastify-alternative  /screencastify-alternative/  301
/webcam-overlay-presentation  /webcam-overlay-presentation/  301
/screen-recorder-for-teachers  /screen-recorder-for-teachers/  301

# The uninstall survey is not prerendered and not in the sitemap, but it still
# needs its 301: chrome.runtime.setUninstallURL points at the bare path, and
# every extension already in the wild has that URL stored in the browser and
# will keep requesting it forever. The slashed form falls through to the
# catch-all below, and React Router renders the survey.
/uninstall-survey  /uninstall-survey/  301

# Redirect pages.dev traffic to production
https://snaprecorder.pages.dev/*  https://www.snaprecorder.org/:splat  301

# ── The catch-all. MUST BE LAST ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Everything that is neither a file on disk nor a 301 above lands here: the
# signed-in app, both editors, /v/ share links, and any unknown URL. All of
# them get the SPA shell, and React Router decides what to render.
#
# There are deliberately no per-route 200 rules above this line. They do not
# work (see the header), and while they were present they were also actively
# harmful — the app routes they claimed to serve never reached this catch-all.
/*  /index.html  200
